**Goal** — Read a job's whole sanitized log through its forge API even while sibling jobs run, and distinguish unavailable logs from readable evidence.

**Motivation** — The repair agent spent 38 minutes reconstructing CI locally, asked for the log through `RequestCapability` (refused, as designed), and ended with an empty commit to re-trigger CI.

**Approach** — **Log read (#2657 defect 1).** A new `readJobLog(ref, checkId, deps, cwd)` in `src/forge/job-log.ts` reads one job's whole log: GitHub through `gh api --allow-escape-sequences --hostname <host> repos/<repo>/actions/jobs/<id>/logs` (retried once without the flag when gh reports it as an unknown flag), GitLab through `glab api --hostname <host> projects/<p>/jobs/<id>/trace`. The text comes back with ANSI CSI colour codes and a leading byte-order mark removed and every secret redacted over the whole log. `stripAnsi` moves from `src/forge/ci-failures.ts` into `src/forge/job-log.ts`, which exports it; `ci-failures.ts` imports it from there. Both forges' CI repair excerpts (`readFailedChecks`) read through `readJobLog`, which replaces `excerptOf` and `logUnavailableExcerpt` in `ci-failures.ts`. `FailedCheck` gains `logUnavailable?: true`, set when the log could not be read. The excerpt source changes from the failed steps only (`--log-failed`) to the whole job log.

`reason` is the first non-empty line of the failed call's stderr, ANSI-stripped, redacted and cut to `CI_EXCERPT_CHARS` characters; `"unknown"` when stderr has no non-empty line.

Failure handling: `gh api` exits non-zero with stderr naming `--allow-escape-sequences` as an unknown flag → `readJobLog` runs the same argv once without the flag and uses that result. Job log read fails for any other reason (including a subprocess timeout) → `readJobLog` returns `{ ok: false, reason }`; the excerpt is `(log unavailable: <reason>)` and `FailedCheck.logUnavailable` is `true`.

Update the existing GitHub fixture migrations exactly: ci-failures.test.ts's githubFixture must distinguish a gh api endpoint ending in actions/jobs/<id>/logs and return that id's log, while other gh api calls still return check-runs. Replace the expected job 991 argv with gh api --allow-escape-sequences --hostname github.com repos/o/r/actions/jobs/991/logs. In ci-failures-gitlab.test.ts replace ghJobLog's joined argv for jobs 991/992 only; scripted replies and expected excerpts are unchanged. The invariant that a failed Actions check's excerpt is built from that job's own log (AC-15 and its neighbours) is unchanged.

**Scope** — In: new job-log reader, sanitization, single flag fallback, failure reason, FailedCheck marker, both CI excerpt call sites, forge barrel exports and fixture migration. Out: annotation-led excerpts (US-002), wait/repair eligibility (US-003), head check listing (US-004), agent log tool (US-007). Job log reads use the forge module's default subprocess timeout; no separate log-read timeout is added.

**Interface** — `src/forge/job-log.ts` (export through `src/forge/index.ts`):
```ts
export type JobLogRead = { readonly ok: true; readonly text: string } | { readonly ok: false; readonly reason: string };
export async function readJobLog(ref: PrRef, checkId: string, deps: ForgeDeps, cwd: string): Promise<JobLogRead>;
export function stripAnsi(text: string): string;
export const CI_ANNOTATION_LINES = 20;
export const CI_ANNOTATION_CHARS = 2_000;
```
`FailedCheck` target: `{ name; url?; excerpt; logUnavailable?: true }`.

**Goal** — List every check of one head commit on GitHub or GitLab with normalized status and job-log availability.

**Motivation** — An agent needs to search the log, not read its tail; that is what the `log` tool does.

**Approach** — **Check listing.** A new `listHeadChecks(ref, headSha, deps, cwd)` in `src/forge/checks-read.ts` lists every check of a head commit, running ones included, with a normalized status (table below). It reads one page of at most 100 check runs (GitHub) or 100 jobs (GitLab). It reuses `readCiListing` and `asRecord`, which `src/forge/ci-failures.ts` exports for use inside the forge module (not from the barrel), so a failed or unparseable GitLab listing fails exactly like the GitHub one.

| Forge status | `ForgeCheck.status` | `conclusion` |
|---|---|---|
| GitHub `completed` | `completed` | the run's `conclusion`, or `"unknown"` when null |
| GitHub `in_progress` | `in_progress` | absent |
| GitHub `queued`, `waiting`, `requested`, `pending`, or any other value | `queued` | absent |
| GitLab `running` | `in_progress` | absent |
| GitLab `success`, `failed`, `canceled`, `skipped` | `completed` | the job's status |
| GitLab `created`, `pending`, `preparing`, `waiting_for_resource`, `scheduled`, `manual`, or any other value | `queued` | absent |

A check run or job without a numeric id is not listed. All of the above are exported from `src/forge/index.ts`.

Mirror the existing MR pipelines lookup for headSha, returning [] if there is no head pipeline. Read `projects/<encoded-project>/pipelines/<id>/jobs?per_page=100` with no failure scope; this all-check listing is distinct from readFailedChecks and its allow_failure/failure-only filtering. Preserve FORGE_CI_READ_FAILED on subprocess, JSON and malformed-listing failures.

**Scope** — In: all-check normalization for both forges, internal listing helper exports, public ForgeCheck exports. Out: reading logs (US-001), tool rendering/head authorization (US-006/007). Check listings read one page: at most 100 GitHub check runs or 100 GitLab jobs of the head's pipeline.

**Interface** — `src/forge/checks-read.ts`:
```ts
export type ForgeCheckStatus = "queued" | "in_progress" | "completed";
export interface ForgeCheck {
  readonly id: string;
  readonly name: string;
  readonly status: ForgeCheckStatus;
  /** Present only when status is "completed". */
  readonly conclusion?: string;
  readonly url?: string;
  /** True for a GitHub Actions job or a GitLab job: readJobLog can read it. */
  readonly hasJobLog: boolean;
}
export async function listHeadChecks(ref: PrRef, headSha: string, deps: ForgeDeps, cwd: string): Promise<readonly ForgeCheck[]>;
```

**Goal** — Establish the PR-bound static provider, forge dependency seam, safe bounded result assembly and shared instructions for downstream read tools.

**Motivation** — Agents get forge reads, not forge writes: creating PRs, posting comments and marking ready stay with nax, which already does them at fixed points; an agent write would bypass the writer gate that decides which PR text becomes fix work.

**Approach** — **The provider.** A new module `src/forge-tools/` builds a static `ToolProvider` with id `nax-forge`, `kind: "static"` and `stages: ["*"]`. Its tools are `pr`, `checks`, `log`, `threads` and `comments`, advertised as `nax-forge__<name>` (and served to ACP Claude as `mcp__nax__nax-forge__<name>`). The PR comes from a `resolveRef` the caller supplies; no tool input names a repo, PR, head or URL. Every tool is read-only and never throws: a failure is an `isError` result. Every forge call the module makes, PR resolution from a URL included, goes through the exported `_forgeToolsDeps` object, the module's test seam.

**Untrusted text and the output budget.** Everything a tool returns that came from the forge (PR fields, check names, log lines, comment bodies) is rendered inside one `markdownFence` block per tool result; the lines nax writes itself (headers, counts, footers) stay outside it. Every tool builds its result with `fitToolResult`, which fits whole entries (a log line, a check line, a comment) to the run context's `maxBytes` instead of cutting the finished string: it keeps the header, as many whole entries as fit from the kept side (`"end"` for the `log` tail view, `"start"` for every other view), always closes the fence, keeps the footer, and adds a `[truncated: <k> entries not shown]` line after the footer when it dropped any. A single entry that does not fit alone is cut on a code-point boundary.

`fitToolResult` renders the header lines, then the entries joined by newlines inside one `markdownFence` block, then the footer lines. `src/prompts/builders/forge-read-builder.ts` exports `buildForgeReadInstructions(): string`, exported from `src/prompts/index.ts`; the provider's `instructions()` returns it.

Every tool takes the bound PR from `resolveRef`, which the provider calls at most once per successful resolution (a rejected resolution is retried on the next call). Every input is validated before mode selection and before any forge call.

Failure handling: `resolveRef` rejects, or the bound URL carries no PR number → the tool returns `isError` naming the reason; no other forge call. A forge read inside a tool throws → the tool returns `isError` with the first non-empty line of the error's message, redacted. Preserve the exact short error content asserted by downstream tools; successful forge payloads use the fenced entry renderer. Share successful/in-flight reference resolution per instance and evict rejected resolutions; binding.cwd/deps determine all forge context independently of the tool run root. The URL helper extracts prNumberFromUrl and resolves via `_forgeToolsDeps.resolvePrRef(number, deps, cwd)`.

**Scope** — In: module/barrel/provider assembly, bindings, resolver/error wrapper, all forge seam fields, UTF-8 whole-entry budget algorithm and instructions builder/barrel export. Out: concrete pr/checks handlers (US-006), log handler/views (US-007/008), writer-only tools (US-009/010), dispatch and attachment (US-011/012). Forge writes are not tools: creating or editing PRs, posting or editing comments, replying to or resolving threads, and marking a PR ready stay with nax's own workflow and bot steps. A configured MCP server whose id is `nax-forge` is not detected as a collision.

**Interface** — `src/forge-tools/` (barrel `index.ts`):
```ts
export const FORGE_READ_PROVIDER_ID = "nax-forge";
export interface ForgeReadBinding {
  readonly resolveRef: () => Promise<PrRef>;
  readonly deps: ForgeDeps;
  readonly cwd: string;
}
export function createForgeReadProvider(binding: ForgeReadBinding): ToolProvider;
export function forgeReadProviderForUrl(url: string, deps: ForgeDeps, cwd: string): ToolProvider;
/** The test seam: every forge call the tools make goes through these fields. */
export const _forgeToolsDeps: {
  resolvePrRef: typeof resolvePrRef;
  readPrInfo: typeof readPrInfo;
  listHeadChecks: typeof listHeadChecks;
  readJobLog: typeof readJobLog;
  listReviewThreads: typeof listReviewThreads;
  listPrComments: typeof listPrComments;
  hasWriteAccess: typeof hasWriteAccess;
};
export interface ToolResultParts {
  readonly header: readonly string[];
  readonly entries: readonly string[];
  readonly footer: readonly string[];
}
export function fitToolResult(parts: ToolResultParts, maxBytes: number, keep: "start" | "end"): string;
export const FORGE_LOG_DEFAULT_LINES = 200;
export const FORGE_LOG_MAX_LINES = 500;
export const FORGE_LOG_MAX_MATCHES = 50;
export const FORGE_LOG_LINE_CHARS = 1_000;
export const FORGE_COMMENT_CHARS = 2_000;
export const FORGE_MAX_THREADS = 50;
export const FORGE_MAX_COMMENTS = 50;
```
```ts
export function buildForgeReadInstructions(): string;
```
